

 Below lists ideas on how TSO emulation on AARCH64 can be sped up on non-TSO hardware w/o HW assist

  --- Disable Thread Migration ---

 Here are patterns where a JIT can elide store barriers:

 1. Inside a critical section 
    - But can you truly identify it from an instruction sequence on AARCH64

 2. NT (Streaming Stores) They are are weakly orderered on x86 as well
 
 3. Any code boundaries that the JIT compiler can do escape analysis on and prove function-region locality

 4. Stack stores
 
 5. For all else:
    - use regular stores and per-core page tables
    - Using COW/ZFOD, OS will allocate pages by default only on one core, the other core will be empty
    - when taking a page trap, check if the page is allocated on another core
      - if yes, send User mode a special trap
      - JIT should handle this, and use reasonable judgement to add store barriers around the code in question and adjoining super block
        that could be using the same page
       -- Thus we are identifying code regions that have ever accessed Shared memory (pages accessed by more than one core)

